{"schema_version": 4, "name": "phenotype-conversion", "title": "IFNg secretion and PDL1n->PDL1p conversion", "created": "2026-09-08", "status": "complete", "design_status": "complete", "implementation_status": "complete", "simulation_status": "ran", "phase": "Evaluate", "question": "Do active T cells reliably produce the IFNg field that drives tumor phenotype conversion,\nand does the downstream PDL1n->PDL1p conversion itself hold at this scale?\n", "study_card": {"goal": "Separate the robust cytokine driver (IFNg secretion) from the slower, scale-limited conversion it causes.", "mechanism": "T cells engaging MHCI+ tumors secrete IFNg into a diffusing field; tumors that internalize enough IFNg switch PDL1n->PDL1p.", "expected_result": "A measurable IFNg field forms only with active T cells (every seed); PDL1p conversion is directionally higher with T cells but slow at 10 h.", "main_expert_question": "What run length / cell count is needed for the PDL1p conversion fraction to separate from growth noise?"}, "biological_summary": "Tumor phenotype conversion is the paper's central mechanism (Hickey, Agmon et al., Cell\nSystems 2024): IFNg secreted by engaged CD8+ T cells diffuses through the tissue and drives\nproliferative PDL1n (MHC-I-low) tumor cells into an arrested, inflammatory PDL1p (MHC-I-high)\nstate that stops dividing (negative for cell-cycle markers, G0). The paper shows this\nconversion \u2014 not the number of kills \u2014 is what controls tumor growth, and that more-active\n(25% PD1+) T cells convert tumors earlier (~18 h) and more strongly than exhausted (75% PD1+)\nT cells. This study isolates the cytokine driver: across 6 seeds active T cells built a\nmeasurable IFNg field in every run while the no-T control stayed at zero \u2014 a robust\nreproduction of the secretion step. The downstream PDL1p fraction is directionally higher\nwith T cells; at the tractable ~10 h scale it does not fully separate from growth noise (it\ndoes at larger scale \u2014 see 